 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
 *   **Mach Bands:** Linear interpolation of integrated opacity $\alpha$ across the polygon causes derivative discontinuities in $\alpha$ due to the non-linear exponential, resulting in disturbing Mach bands [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Using the 2D texture lookup avoids this [S3, unofficial].
 *   **Incorrect Color Averaging:** If both color and extinction are interpolated linearly across a ray segment, the term $a \cdot C_{polygon}$ yields incorrect color because it averages colors before applying opacity, failing to account for the obscuring effect of high-opacity regions (e.g., red obscuring green) [S1]. The method approximates the correct color by computing it analytically at vertices and dividing by $\alpha$ [S1].
-*   **Perspective View Approximation:** In perspective view, $\tau_f$ and $\tau_b$ have different perspective distortions, and correct $l$ computation requires a square root per pixel. The method is an approximation in perspective, interpolating $\tau$ and color in screen space [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Perspective View Approximation:** In perspective view, $\tau_f$ and $\tau_b$ have different perspective distortions, and correct $l$ computation requires a square root per pixel [S2, unofficial]. The method is an approximation in perspective, interpolating $\tau$ and color in screen space [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Incremental Method Robustness:** The incremental method for general cells is slow and not completely robust because it uses floating-point computations to determine topology [S3, unofficial].
 *   **Wrapping:** If texture coordinates are not clamped, they may wrap around, causing incorrect transparency values [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the visibility sort of cells and the rasterization of polygons [S3, unofficial]. The per-pixel cost is reduced to a texture lookup and compositing operation, avoiding per-pixel exponentials [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the 2D texture map. The size of the texture map depends on the scaling of $u$ and $v$ [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. [S1]
-*   **Scaling:** The method scales with the number of cells and the resolution of the image. The use of hardware texture mapping allows for efficient rendering on GPUs [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Avoids per-pixel exponential calculations; prevents Mach bands; leverages hardware acceleration [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Approximate in perspective view; color computation is approximate (analytic integral at vertices); incremental subdivision is slow and potentially unstable for general cells [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When hardware acceleration is available and exponential transparency is required to avoid artifacts [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Suitable for orthogonal projections and approximate perspective views. [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]
